{
  "image_name": "001e22a7d0e2169b3649dfab5f7f588d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how epithelial tissues are organized and what they do, while comparing vertebrate and insect epithelia. Across the top, a simple epithelial sheet is shown performing several key roles: it acts as a barrier against pathogens, supports exocytosis or secretion at the apical surface, can bend or fold to shape tissues, and can undergo EMT, where epithelial cells lose their orderly arrangement and move away from the layer. The drawing also highlights the basic polarity of an epithelial cell, with apical, lateral, and basal domains and a tight junction near the top.\n\nThe lower part compares junctional architecture in vertebrates versus insects. Both epithelia have apical microvilli and are anchored basally to a basement membrane, but their cell–cell junctions differ. In vertebrates, the apical region includes tight junctions made of proteins such as JAM, occludin, and claudin, with adherens junctions below containing cadherin and nectin. In insects, adherens junctions lie apically and include cadherin and echinoid, while the main occluding junction is a septate junction composed of proteins including Megatrachea, FasIII, and Sinuous. Cytoskeletal elements such as actin and spectrin are also indicated, emphasizing conserved polarity with distinct molecular solutions.",
